s activity modification and physiotherapy techniques like postural correction and mobilization. Surgical options include rib resection and vascular reconstruction. The conclusion emphasizes that treatment depends on the TOS subtype, with rehabilitation being essential and vascular emergencies requiring urgent care.\n\n\n**Defining Syndrome Content**\n\nI'm now defining the specifics of 3 VERTEBROBASILAR SYNDROME, including its definition, causes, pathophysiology, and clinical features. I am specifically focusing on the critical red flag presentation, which is vertigo associated with focal neurological deficits, and the list of clinical symptoms. My focus is on cataloging details in an organized manner.\n\n\n**Outlining VBS Treatment**\n\nI'm now outlining treatment for Vertebrobasilar Syndrome (VBS). I'm focusing on investigations, noting MRI and CT protocols alongside vascular studies and ECG for embolic sources. I'm also cataloging the list of potential complications, including stroke and neurological deficits, and defining Conservative and Physiotherapy management methods.\n\n\n**Expanding Syndrome Focus**\n\nI'm now detailing Scalene Syndrome, starting with its definition as a thoracic outlet compression within the interscalene triangle. I'm noting causes like muscle issues and postural problems, and the impact of trauma. The aim is to thoroughly document this section.\n\n\n**Describing Scalene Symptoms**\n\nI'm now detailing Scalene Syndrome's clinical presentation, investigations, complications, and conservative management. I'm noting the sequence from structural issues to symptoms, along with key clinical features and diagnostic tools. Finally, I'm noting potential complications and conservative treatments.\n\n\n**Defining Syndrome Components**\n\nI'm now detailing Costoclavicular Syndrome. I've defined it as a thoracic outlet syndrome caused by compression between the clavicle and first rib. I'm focusing on the causes, including posture, deformity, muscle tightness, and trauma, and am beginning to review the pathophysiology.\n\n\n**Defining Syndrome Management**\n\nI'm now detailing Costoclavicular Syndrome's clinical features, noting the neurological and vascular symptoms. I'm focusing on investigations such as X-rays, Doppler ultrasound, and angiography. I'm also now detailing the complications, followed by outlining the Conservative and Surgical Management strategies.\n\n\n**Defining Syndrome Features**\n\nI'm now focusing on Levator Scapulae Syndrome (LSS), beginning with its definition. I'm noting causes such as posture and overuse, then delving into the pathophysiology, specifically how prolonged loading can lead to pain. I'm aiming to capture all the key components systematically.\n\n\n**Detailing Syndrome Analysis**\n\nI'm now detailing Levator Scapulae Syndrome (LSS)'s clinical features. I'm noting pain presentation locations. I'm also reviewing investigations, complications and management strategies; including both conservative treatments and the surgical options. My focus is on thoroughly detailing the diagnosis and treatment protocols.\n\n\n**Defining Piriformis Details**\n\nI'm now detailing Piriformis Syndrome. I've noted its definition, causes including anatomical variations and overuse, and pathophysiology, specifically the nerve compression leading to pain. I'm noting key clinical features such as deep buttock pain and radiating symptoms. Finally, if symptoms are due to an underlying structural cervical or shoulder disorder treatment is directed toward the underlying pathology; most patients respond well to postural correction.\n\n\n**Detailing Piriformis Analysis**\n\nI'm now detailing the clinical features of Piriformis Syndrome, focusing on symptoms like paraesthesia, pain from prolonged sitting, and tenderness. I'm noting specific special tests such as FAIR, Freiberg, Pace, and Beatty manoeuvre. Also being detailed, is the differential diagnosis, investigations, and possible complications, alongside conservative management options.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":27,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T11:33:16Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n1. CEREBRAL PALSY Definition Cerebral palsy is a group of permanent disorders of movement and posture causing activity limitation, attributed to non-progressive disturbances occurring in the developing fetal or infant brain. It may be accompanied by disturbances of sensation, perception, cognition, communication, behaviour and epilepsy. Causes / risk factors • Prenatal: congenital brain malformations, intrauterine infections, genetic abnormalities, maternal diabetes or hypertension and placental abnormalities • Perinatal: prematurity, low birth weight, hypoxic-ischaemic injury, intracranial haemorrhage and neonatal seizures • Postnatal: meningitis, encephalitis, head injury, cerebrovascular injury, severe hypoxia, kernicterus and hypoglycaemia Classification • Spastic CP: hemiplegia, diplegia and quadriplegia • Dyskinetic CP: dystonia and choreoathetosis • Ataxic CP: poor balance, incoordination and tremor • Mixed CP: combination of motor patterns Clinical features • Delayed motor milestones • Abnormal muscle tone: spasticity, dystonia or hypotonia • Weakness and poor selective motor control • Abnormal reflexes and persistence of primitive reflexes • Abnormal gait and poor balance • Contractures and deformities • Hip displacement, scoliosis and foot deformities • Seizures • Visual/hearing impairment • Speech, communication, feeding or swallowing difficulties • Cognitive or behavioural difficulties in some individuals Complications • Contractures • Joint deformities • Hip subluxation/dislocation • Scoliosis • Osteopenia • Pain • Reduced mobility • Epilepsy • Feeding and respiratory problems • Reduced independence and participation Investigations• Neurological and developmental assessment • MRI brain • EEG when seizures are suspected • Hip radiographs when indicated • Spine radiographs when indicated • Vision and hearing assessment • Functional assessment Medical management • Individualized multidisciplinary management • Spasticity: oral antispastic medications in selected patients • Botulinum toxin for focal spasticity • Intrathecal baclofen in selected severe cases • Antiepileptic medication when required • Pain, feeding, constipation, bladder, nutrition and respiratory management Surgical management • Tendon lengthening and tendon transfer • Osteotomy and correction of hip displacement • Foot reconstruction • Spinal surgery for severe scoliosis • Selective dorsal rhizotomy in carefully selected children with severe lower-limb spasticity • Intrathecal baclofen pump in selected severe generalized spasticity Physiotherapy management • Promote motor development and functional movement • Positioning and seating for alignment • Passive/active ROM and stretching • Strengthening and weight-bearing activities • Postural control and balance training • Gait training with appropriate aids • Orthoses when indicated • Transfer, ADL and mobility training • Respiratory physiotherapy when required • Home exercise and caregiver education Multidisciplinary approach • Paediatrician/neurologist • Orthopaedic surgeon • Physiotherapist • Occupational therapist • Speech and language therapist • Orthotist• Psychologist • Special educator • Dietitian • Social worker and rehabilitation physician Conclusion Cerebral palsy is a lifelong condition requiring early, individualized and multidisciplinary rehabilitation. Management aims to maximize mobility, independence, participation and quality of life while preventing secondary complications.2. POLIOMYELITIS Definition Poliomyelitis is an acute viral infectious disease caused by poliovirus, primarily affecting motor neurons of the anterior horn of the spinal cord and sometimes the brainstem, resulting in acute flaccid paralysis. Causative organism and transmission • Poliovirus is an enterovirus of the Picornaviridae family • Transmission is mainly feco-oral through contaminated food and water • Oral-oral transmission may also occur Pathogenesis • Virus enters through gastrointestinal or respiratory tract • Replicates in pharynx and intestine • May enter bloodstream • In some individuals reaches the CNS • Motor neurons of anterior horn may be destroyed • This produces lower motor neuron weakness and flaccid paralysis Clinical types • Inapparent infection • Abortive poliomyelitis: fever, malaise, headache, sore throat, nausea/vomiting • Non-paralytic poliomyelitis: meningitic symptoms such as headache, neck stiffness and muscle pain • Paralytic poliomyelitis: acute flaccid, often asymmetrical paralysis with reduced/absent reflexes • Bulbar poliomyelitis: dysphagia, dysarthria, respiratory difficulty and cranial nerve dysfunction Clinical features • Fever, fatigue and headache • Nausea/vomiting • Muscle pain • Acute flaccid weakness • Asymmetrical paralysis • Reduced muscle tone • Reduced/absent deep tendon reflexes • Muscle wasting • Respiratory and swallowing weakness in severe disease • Sensation is generally preserved Complications • Respiratory failure • Aspiration • Permanent paralysis • Contractures • Joint deformities • Scoliosis• Limb-length discrepancy • Osteoporosis • Gait abnormalities • Post-polio syndrome Investigations • Clinical neurological examination • Stool testing for poliovirus • Throat specimens when appropriate • CSF examination • Electromyography • Nerve conduction studies • Respiratory function assessment when required Medical management • There is no specific treatment that reverses established paralytic poliomyelitis • Supportive care, hydration, nutrition and pain/fever management • Respiratory support and assisted ventilation when required • Airway clearance • Swallowing management • Prevention and treatment of complications Surgical management • Surgery does not treat the acute viral infection • For residual deformity: tendon transfer, tendon lengthening, osteotomy, contracture correction, foot reconstruction and spinal surgery for severe scoliosis Physiotherapy management • Acute: positioning, gentle passive ROM, breathing exercises, pressure-relieving measures and appropriate splinting • Avoid excessive exercise of acutely denervated muscles • Recovery: active-assisted and gradual active exercise, muscle re-education and strengthening of useful innervated muscles • Residual: gait training, orthoses, functional independence and energy conservation • Post-polio syndrome: pacing, energy conservation, appropriate low/moderate exercise and avoidance of overuse Prevention • Vaccination with IPV and/or OPV according to the applicable immunization programme • Safe water and sanitation • Hand hygiene • Surveillance and outbreak control Conclusion Poliomyelitis can produce permanent motor disability through anterior horn motor-neuron injury. Supportive medical care, prevention of secondary complications, physiotherapy, orthotic management and reconstructive surgery when necessary are central to rehabilitation.3. SPINAL DYSRAPHISM Definition Spinal dysraphism is a group of congenital disorders caused by abnormal development and incomplete closure of neural tube and/or vertebral structures, producing defects involving the spinal cord, meninges, vertebrae and overlying tissues. Classification • Open dysraphism: neural tissue is exposed or incompletely covered • Myelomeningocele: spinal cord and meninges protrude through a vertebral defect • Myelocele: neural tissue is directly exposed • Closed dysraphism: skin-covered defects such as spina bifida occulta, meningocele, lipomyelomeningocele, tethered cord and dermal sinus Causes / risk factors • Multifactorial origin • Folate deficiency • Maternal nutritional deficiency • Certain maternal medications • Maternal diabetes • Genetic susceptibility • Previous pregnancy affected by neural tube defect • Environmental factors Clinical features • Lower-limb weakness or paralysis • Flaccid or spastic paralysis depending on lesion • Reduced sensation • Altered reflexes • Bladder and bowel dysfunction • Hip dislocation • Contractures • Clubfoot and other foot deformities • Knee deformities • Scoliosis/kyphosis • Hydrocephalus • Chiari II malformation, especially with myelomeningocele • Tethered cord • Pressure sores Complications • Hydrocephalus • Neurological deterioration • Tethered cord syndrome• Recurrent urinary tract infection • Renal damage • Bowel dysfunction • Contractures and deformities • Pressure sores • Scoliosis • Reduced mobility • Psychosocial difficulties Investigations • Prenatal ultrasound and maternal screening • Fetal MRI in selected cases • Postnatal MRI spine • Ultrasound/CT in selected cases • Neurological examination • Urodynamic studies • Renal function assessment • Orthopaedic assessment Medical management • Hydrocephalus management • Bladder and bowel management • Prevention of infection • Skin care • Nutritional support • Pain management • Management of associated neurological problems Surgical management • Early closure of myelomeningocele to protect neural tissue • Hydrocephalus: ventriculoperitoneal shunt or endoscopic third ventriculostomy in selected cases • Tethered cord release when clinically indicated • Orthopaedic procedures: tendon procedures, osteotomy, foot correction, hip procedures and spinal deformity correction Physiotherapy management • Protect surgical wound and prevent pressure sores • Proper positioning and gentle ROM • Facilitate motor development • Improve head and trunk control • Strengthen available muscles • Encourage age-appropriate mobility • Stretching, splinting and orthotic management• Sitting and standing balance • Gait training when possible • Wheelchair and transfer skills • ADL and community mobility training Bladder and bowel rehabilitation • Timed voiding • Clean intermittent catheterization when prescribed • Bowel programme • Skin care • Patient and family education Multidisciplinary approach • Neurosurgeon • Paediatrician • Urologist • Orthopaedic surgeon • Physiotherapist • Occupational therapist • Orthotist • Rehabilitation physician • Nurse • Psychologist and social worker Conclusion Spinal dysraphism can cause neurological, orthopaedic and urological disability. Early surgical management combined with lifelong multidisciplinary rehabilitation maximizes independence and prevents secondary complications.4. LEPROSY Definition Leprosy (Hansen disease) is a chronic infectious disease caused mainly by Mycobacterium leprae, primarily affecting the skin, peripheral nerves, mucosa of the upper respiratory tract and eyes. Causative organism and transmission • Mycobacterium leprae is an acid-fast bacillus • Primarily affects Schwann cells and macrophages • Transmission is associated with prolonged close contact with untreated infectious disease, particularly respiratory droplets • It is not spread by casual brief contact Classification • WHO operational classification: paucibacillary (PB) and multibacillary (MB) • Ridley-Jopling spectrum: indeterminate, tuberculoid, borderline tuberculoid, mid-borderline, borderline lepromatous and lepromatous Clinical features • Hypopigmented or reddish skin patches • Definite sensory loss over lesions • Dry skin and loss of sweating • Nodules in some forms • Loss of hair over lesions • Thickened peripheral nerves with sensory/motor deficit • Paraesthesia and numbness • Muscle weakness and wasting • Foot drop • Claw hand • Lagophthalmos • Recurrent ulcers and burns Common nerves affected • Ulnar nerve • Median nerve • Radial nerve • Common peroneal nerve • Posterior tibial nerve • Facial nerve Complications • Peripheral neuropathy • Sensory loss and paralysis • Contractures • Claw hand• Foot drop • Muscle wasting • Recurrent ulcers • Burns and secondary infection • Corneal anaesthesia and ulceration • Visual impairment • Stigma and social/occupational difficulties Investigations • Complete skin examination • Sensory testing • Peripheral nerve examination • Motor examination • Slit-skin smear when indicated • Skin biopsy • Eye examination • Functional assessment Medical management • Multidrug therapy (MDT) is the cornerstone • Common antileprosy drugs: rifampicin, dapsone and clofazimine • Regimen and duration depend on classification and current national/WHO guidance • Early treatment helps prevent nerve damage and disability Management of reactions • Type 1 reaction may cause inflammation and acute nerve impairment • Type 2 reaction may cause fever, painful nodules and systemic inflammation • Corticosteroids and other specialist-directed treatment may be required depending on the reaction Surgical management • Selected nerve decompression • Tendon transfer for claw hand • Tendon transfer for foot drop • Contracture correction • Reconstructive procedures • Ulcer management • Eyelid procedures for lagophthalmos Physiotherapy management • Assess sensation, muscle power, ROM, deformity, skin integrity, balance and gait • Daily skin inspection and protective footwear • Avoid extreme heat and barefoot walking • ROM, stretching and strengthening of functioning muscles • Balance and functional training• Claw hand: splinting, ROM, strengthening and post-tendon-transfer rehabilitation • Foot drop: AFO, gait training and balance training • Ulcer prevention: pressure relief, off-loading, appropriate footwear and education Multidisciplinary approach • Dermatologist/physician • Neurologist • Physiotherapist • Occupational therapist • Orthopaedic/plastic surgeon • Ophthalmologist • Foot-care services where available • Psychologist and social worker Prevention • Early diagnosis • Prompt MDT treatment • Contact examination/surveillance • Appropriate preventive strategies for eligible contacts • Health education • Early recognition of nerve involvement • Disability prevention and self-care education Conclusion Leprosy is a chronic infectious disease with important neurological and disability-related consequences. Early diagnosis and MDT prevent progression, while physiotherapy, orthoses, reconstructive surgery and self-care education help prevent disability and maintain function.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-13T17:03:16+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
