on foot reconstruction and spinal surgery for severe scoliosis **Physiotherapy management** • Acute positioning gentle passive ROM breathing exercises pressure relieving measures and appropriate splinting • Avoid excessive exercise of acutely denervated muscles • Recovery active assisted and gradual active exercise muscle re education and strengthening of useful innervated muscles • Residual gait training orthoses functional independence and energy conservation • Post polio syndrome pacing energy conservation appropriate low/moderate exercise and avoidance of overuse **Prevention** • Vaccination with IPV and/or OPV according to the applicable immunization programme • Safe water and sanitation • Hand hygiene • Surveillance and outbreak control **Conclusion** Poliomyelitis can produce permanent motor disability through anterior horn motor neuron injury Supportive medical care prevention of secondary complications physiotherapy orthotic management and reconstructive surgery when necessary are central to rehabilitation --- **3 SPINAL DYSRAPHISM** **Definition** Spinal dysraphism is a group of congenital disorders caused by abnormal development and incomplete closure of neural tube and/or vertebral structures producing defects involving the spinal cord meninges vertebrae and overlying tissues **Classification** • Open dysraphism neural tissue is exposed or incompletely covered • Myelomeningocele spinal cord and meninges protrude through a vertebral defect • Myelocele neural tissue is directly exposed • Closed dysraphism skin covered defects such as spina bifida occulta meningocele lipomyelomeningocele tethered cord and dermal sinus **Causes / risk factors** • Multifactorial origin • Folate deficiency • Maternal nutritional deficiency • Certain maternal medications • Maternal diabetes • Genetic susceptibility • Previous pregnancy affected by neural tube defect • Environmental factors **Clinical features** • Lower limb weakness or paralysis • Flaccid or spastic paralysis depending on lesion • Reduced sensation • Altered reflexes • Bladder and bowel dysfunction • Hip dislocation • Contractures • Clubfoot and other foot deformities • Knee deformities • Scoliosis/kyphosis • Hydrocephalus • Chiari II malformation especially with myelomeningocele • Tethered cord • Pressure sores **Complications** • Hydrocephalus • Neurological deterioration • Tethered cord syndrome • Recurrent urinary tract infection • Renal damage • Bowel dysfunction • Contractures and deformities • Pressure sores • Scoliosis • Reduced mobility • Psychosocial difficulties **Investigations** • Prenatal ultrasound and maternal screening • Fetal MRI in selected cases • Postnatal MRI spine • Ultrasound/CT in selected cases • Neurological examination • Urodynamic studies • Renal function assessment • Orthopaedic assessment **Medical management** • Hydrocephalus management • Bladder and bowel management • Prevention of infection • Skin care • Nutritional support • Pain management • Management of associated neurological problems **Surgical management** • Early closure of myelomeningocele to protect neural tissue • Hydrocephalus ventriculoperitoneal shunt or endoscopic third ventriculostomy in selected cases • Tethered cord release when clinically indicated • Orthopaedic procedures tendon procedures osteotomy foot correction hip procedures and spinal deformity correction **Physiotherapy management** • Protect surgical wound and prevent pressure sores • Proper positioning and gentle ROM • Facilitate motor development • Improve head and trunk control • Strengthen available muscles • Encourage age appropriate mobility • Stretching splinting and orthotic management • Sitting and standing balance • Gait training when possible • Wheelchair and transfer skills • ADL and community mobility training **Bladder and bowel rehabilitation** • Timed voiding • Clean intermittent catheterization when prescribed • Bowel programme • Skin care • Patient and family education **Multidisciplinary approach** • Neurosurgeon • Paediatrician • Urologist • Orthopaedic surgeon • Physiotherapist • Occupational therapist • Orthotist • Rehabilitation physician • Nurse • Psychologist and social worker **Conclusion** Spinal dysraphism can cause neurological orthopaedic and urological disability Early surgical management combined with lifelong multidisciplinary rehabilitation maximizes independence and prevents secondary complications --- **4 LEPROSY** **Definition** Leprosy Hansen disease is a chronic infectious disease caused mainly by Mycobacterium leprae primarily affecting the skin peripheral nerves mucosa of the upper respiratory tract and eyes **Causative organism and transmission** • Mycobacterium leprae is an acid fast bacillus • Primarily affects Schwann cells and macrophages • Transmission is associated with prolonged close contact with untreated infectious disease particularly respiratory droplets • It is not spread by casual brief contact **Classification** • WHO operational classification paucibacillary PB and multibacillary MB • Ridley Jopling spectrum indeterminate tuberculoid borderline tuberculoid mid borderline borderline lepromatous and lepromatous **Clinical features** • Hypopigmented or reddish skin patches • Definite sensory loss over lesions • Dry skin and loss of sweating • Nodules in some forms • Loss of hair over lesions • Thickened peripheral nerves with sensory/motor deficit • Paraesthesia and numbness • Muscle weakness and wasting • Foot drop • Claw hand • Lagophthalmos • Recurrent ulcers and burns **Common nerves affected** • Ulnar nerve • Median nerve • Radial nerve • Common peroneal nerve • Posterior tibial nerve • Facial nerve **Complications** • Peripheral neuropathy • Sensory loss and paralysis • Contractures • Claw hand • Foot drop • Muscle wasting • Recurrent ulcers • Burns and secondary infection • Corneal anaesthesia and ulceration • Visual impairment • Stigma and social/occupational difficulties **Investigations** • Complete skin examination • Sensory testing • Peripheral nerve examination • Motor examination • Slit skin smear when indicated • Skin biopsy • Eye examination • Functional assessment **Medical management** • Multidrug therapy MDT is the cornerstone • Common antileprosy drugs rifampicin dapsone and clofazimine • Regimen and duration depend on classification and current national/WHO guidance • Early treatment helps prevent nerve damage and disability **Management of reactions** • Type 1 reaction may cause inflammation and acute nerve impairment • Type 2 reaction may cause fever painful nodules and systemic inflammation • Corticosteroids and other specialist directed treatment may be required depending on the reaction **Surgical management** • Selected nerve decompression • Tendon transfer for claw hand • Tendon transfer for foot drop • Contracture correction • Reconstructive procedures • Ulcer management • Eyelid procedures for lagophthalmos **Physiotherapy management** • Assess sensation muscle power ROM deformity skin integrity balance and gait • Daily skin inspection and protective footwear • Avoid extreme heat and barefoot walking • ROM stretching and strengthening of functioning muscles • Balance and functional training • Claw hand splinting ROM strengthening and post tendon transfer rehabilitation • Foot drop AFO gait training and balance training • Ulcer prevention pressure relief off loading appropriate footwear and education **Multidisciplinary approach** • Dermatologist/physician • Neurologist • Physiotherapist • Occupational therapist • Orthopaedic/plastic surgeon • Ophthalmologist • Foot care services where available • Psychologist and social worker **Prevention** • Early diagnosis • Prompt MDT treatment • Contact examination/surveillance • Appropriate preventive strategies for eligible contacts • Health education • Early recognition of nerve involvement • Disability prevention and self care education **Conclusion** Leprosy is a chronic infectious disease with important neurological and disability related consequences Early diagnosis and MDT prevent progression while physiotherapy orthoses reconstructive surgery and self care education help prevent disability and maintain function","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing on the most efficient tool utilization. It's becoming clearer that prioritizing specific tools will drastically improve my problem-solving ability. The goal is to always select the tool best suited for the immediate task at hand, regardless of how broad a command may be. This will keep work on the project organized.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Selection**\n\nI'm now prioritizing precise tool selection based on new guidelines. Remembering instruction 1, I am avoiding `cat`, internal `grep`, `ls`, and others for certain tasks. Regarding instruction 2, I'm analyzing the tools needed to remove page numbers embedded in the text. I've identified the use of `sed` as a likely tool and am considering if any other tools will be needed to handle this task.\n\n\n**Defining Cerebral Palsy**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on defining Cerebral Palsy for this project. The focus is on the permanent nature of the movement and posture disorders the limitations they cause and that the cause is a non progressive disturbance during development. It's crucial to acknowledge the possibility of co-occurring sensory perceptual cognitive communication behavioral and epileptic disturbances.\n\n\n**Analyzing CP Factors**\n\nI'm now delving into the causes and classification of Cerebral Palsy. Identifying prenatal, perinatal, and postnatal risk factors is key. Differentiating spastic, dyskinetic, ataxic, and mixed CP types is the next step. I'm also analyzing clinical features and associated complications to gain a broader understanding of the condition.\n\n\n**Outlining Treatments & Assessments**\n\nI'm now outlining treatments and assessment methods for Cerebral Palsy, considering diverse approaches. I'm focusing on the necessity of comprehensive neurological and developmental assessments, as well as imaging techniques such as MRI and EEG. Medical management strategies encompass antispastic medications, Botulinum toxin, and intrathecal baclofen, while also highlighting the importance of managing related complications. Surgical interventions, including tendon lengthening and spinal surgery, are being considered too.\n\n\n**Detailing Physiotherapy & Approach**\n\nI'm currently focusing on the role of physiotherapy in managing Cerebral Palsy. Specifically, I'm examining ways to foster motor development and functional movement, including positioning, range of motion, and strengthening. A multidisciplinary approach is essential, so the involvement of a diverse team, including pediatricians, therapists, and educators, is critical. The key goal is to maximize the patient's mobility, independence, participation, and quality of life while mitigating secondary complications.\n\n\n**Defining Poliomyelitis**\n\nI am now outlining the definition, causative organism, transmission, pathogenesis, and clinical types of Poliomyelitis. Specifically, I'm working to articulate how the poliovirus affects the motor neurons and spinal cord, leading to paralysis. I'm focusing on the different clinical presentations, from inapparent infection to paralytic poliomyelitis, to fully capture the disease's range.\n\n\n**Summarizing Symptoms**\n\nI'm now detailing clinical features and complications of Poliomyelitis. Symptoms such as fever, fatigue, and muscle pain are central. I'm focusing on the acute flaccid weakness, asymmetrical paralysis, and reduced reflexes as indicators. Severe cases lead to respiratory and swallowing weaknesses, while complications include permanent paralysis, contractures, and respiratory failure. I'm also cataloging investigations: clinical examination, stool and throat specimens, CSF analysis, and respiratory function assessment.\n\n\n**Outlining Acute and Long Term Treatments**\n\nI'm now detailing acute and long-term treatments for Poliomyelitis. Assisted ventilation, airway clearance, and swallowing management are crucial acute interventions, along with prevention of complications. Surgical management, focusing on residual deformities through tendon transfers and spinal surgery, is essential. Physiotherapy focuses on acute positioning and breathing exercises, and progresses to gait training. Prevention relies on vaccination, safe sanitation, hand hygiene, and surveillance.\n\n\n**Analyzing Spinal Dysraphism**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on detailing anterior horn motor neuron injury and its related care and treatments. I am outlining supportive medical care, including prevention of secondary complications. Physiotherapy, orthotic management, and reconstructive surgery are also essential elements for rehabilitation. Furthermore, I've began to detail what I'm understanding about Spinal Dysraphism, defining it as a group of congenital disorders linked to abnormal development of the neural tube and/or vertebral structures. The classification includes open and closed types, like myelomeningocele and spina bifida occulta. I'm exploring multifactorial causes, including folate deficiency, maternal nutritional issues, and genetics.\n\n\n**Detailing Clinical Features**\n\nI'm now detailing clinical features and the common complications associated with Spinal Dysraphism. I'm noting the wide range, from lower limb weakness and altered reflexes to bowel and bladder dysfunction, which all depend on the lesion. I'm also cataloging the orthopedic complications, such as clubfoot, and knee/hip deformities. Furthermore, hydrocephalus, tethered cord syndrome, and neurological deterioration are being considered. I'm focusing on the types of investigations and management necessary.\n\n\n\n**Prioritizing Treatments**\n\nI'm now detailing surgical management, emphasizing early myelomeningocele closure to protect neural tissue and manage hydrocephalus and tethered cord issues. I'm also focusing on orthopedic procedures for foot, hip, and spinal deformity corrections. I am moving towards physiotherapy management, focusing on protecting surgical wounds, facilitating motor development and encouraging mobility. Bladder and bowel rehabilitation, like timed voiding and clean intermittent catheterization, are being detailed.\n\n\n**Defining Leprosy**\n\nI'm now outlining key aspects of Leprosy. I'm focusing on the chronic nature of the disease, caused primarily by *Mycobacterium leprae*, and its effect on skin, peripheral nerves, and mucous membranes. The emphasis is on identifying the causative organism and the mode of transmission. Specifically, I'm noting that close, prolonged contact with an untreated individual, particularly through respiratory droplets, is essential for transmission.\n\n\n**Analyzing Leprosy Details**\n\nI am now organizing details on the WHO's operational classification and Ridley-Jopling spectrum for Leprosy, noting the different forms. I'm also now detailing the clinical features, ranging from skin patches and sensory loss to nodule formation and thickened nerves. I am focusing on the commonly affected nerves, like the ulnar and facial nerves, and the complications that arise, from sensory loss to contractures and visual impairments.\n\n\n**Evaluating Treatments**\n\nI'm now investigating leprosy management further. I'm prioritizing investigations, starting with a comprehensive skin exam, sensory testing, and motor assessments, including examining the peripheral nerves. I'm also focusing on medical management, especially multidrug therapy with rifampicin, dapsone, and clofazimine, emphasizing early intervention to prevent nerve damage. I'm detailing management of reactions, including corticosteroids and surgical interventions, and how to improve function.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Rehabilitation Techniques**\n\nI am now focusing on the rehabilitation techniques for leprosy, detailing specific interventions. I'm prioritizing daily skin inspection, protective footwear, and the avoidance of extreme temperatures to prevent ulcers. I am also planning ROM stretching, strengthening of functioning muscles, balance training, and gait analysis. I'm also now detailing the role of splinting and post tendon transfer rehabilitation.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:43:10Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nSPECIAL TESTS — LUMBO-SACRAL REGION I. TEST FOR LUMBAR INSTABILITY 1. PASSIVE LUMBAR EXTENSION TEST PURPOSE To assess lumbar spine instability. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient lies prone. Examiner lifts both the patient’s legs together by holding around the ankles. The legs are elevated while the knees remain extended. Observe the patient’s symptoms and lumbar response. POSITIVE SIGN • • Development/reproduction of low-back pain Feeling of heaviness or instability in the lumbar region. INDICATES Lumbar segmental instability. II. TESTS FOR MUSCLE DYSFUNCTION 2. 90–90 STRAIGHT LEG RAISING TEST PURPOSE To assess hamstring muscle tightness/flexibility. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient lies supine. Flex the hip and knee to approximately 90°. Stabilize the thigh. Slowly extend the knee while maintaining hip flexion. POSITIVE SIGN • • Inability to achieve full knee extension. Increased resistance/tightness in the posterior thigh. INDICATES Hamstring tightness/shortness. 3. TRENDELENBURG TEST PURPOSE To assess hip abductor muscle function, especially gluteus medius. PROCEDURE • • • Patient stands on one leg. Ask the patient to lift the opposite foot from the floor. Observe the position of the pelvis. POSITIVE SIGN • Pelvis drops on the side of the lifted/non-weight-bearing leg. INDICATES Weakness of the hip abductors of the weight-bearing leg, particularly gluteus medius.III. TESTS FOR NEUROLOGICAL DYSFUNCTION 4. STRAIGHT LEG RAISING TEST (SLR/LASÈGUE’S TEST) PURPOSE To assess lumbosacral nerve-root irritation, particularly due to PIVD. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient lies supine. Keep the knee fully extended. Slowly raise the patient’s straight leg by flexing the hip. Note the angle and symptoms produced. POSITIVE SIGN • Reproduction of radicular/sciatic pain, typically between about 30–70° of hip flexion. INDICATES Lumbosacral nerve-root irritation/compression, commonly associated with PIVD. 5. SLUMP TEST PURPOSE To assess neural tissue mechanosensitivity, particularly lumbosacral nerve-root involvement. PROCEDURE Patient sits with: 1. 2. 3. 4. 5. Hands behind back. Thoracic/lumbar spine flexed. Cervical spine flexed. One knee is extended. Ankle is dorsiflexed. The examiner progressively adds these components while observing symptoms. POSITIVE SIGN • • Reproduction of the patient’s familiar neural/radicular symptoms. Symptoms change with release of one component, particularly cervical extension or ankle movement. INDICATES Neural tissue/nerve-root involvement, such as lumbar radiculopathy. IV. TESTS FOR JOINT DYSFUNCTION6. SCHOBER’S TEST PURPOSE To assess lumbar spine flexion/mobility. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient stands erect. Mark the lumbosacral region and a point 10 cm above it. Ask the patient to bend forward maximally. Measure the distance between the marks again. POSITIVE SIGN Insufficient increase in distance during forward flexion. INDICATES Reduced lumbar flexion/mobility, commonly seen in conditions such as ankylosing spondylitis. 7. FABER / PATRICK’S TEST FABER = Flexion – ABduction – External Rotation PURPOSE To assess hip and sacroiliac joint pathology. PROCEDURE • • • • • ◦ ◦ Patient lies supine. Place the tested leg in: Hip flexion Abduction External rotation ◦ Place the lateral ankle over the opposite thigh. Stabilize the opposite pelvis. Gently press the flexed knee downward. POSITIVE SIGN • • • Pain reproduced in the hip/groin → suggests hip pathology. Pain in the sacroiliac region → may indicate SI joint pathology. Restricted ROM may also be significant. INDICATES Hip or sacroiliac joint dysfunction.8. GAENSLEN’S TEST PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint dysfunction/pathology. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient lies near the edge of the table. One hip and knee are maximally flexed toward the chest. The opposite leg is allowed to hang off the edge of the table. Apply gentle pressure to produce opposite movements at the pelvis. POSITIVE SIGN • Reproduction of pain in the sacroiliac region. INDICATES Sacroiliac joint dysfunction. 9. GILLET’S TEST PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint mobility. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient stands. Examiner palpates the PSIS and sacrum. Patient flexes one hip and knee, lifting the foot from the floor. Observe movement of the PSIS relative to the sacrum. POSITIVE SIGN • Abnormal or restricted movement of the PSIS compared with the opposite side. INDICATES Sacroiliac joint hypomobility/dysfunction. 10. MARCH’S TEST PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint movement/dysfunction. PROCEDURE • • • • Patient stands. Examiner palpates the PSIS. Patient marches by lifting one knee at a time. Observe the movement of the PSIS. POSITIVE SIGN • Abnormal/asymmetrical movement of the PSIS. INDICATES Sacroiliac joint dysfunction or altered mobility.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-14T15:13:10+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\n<USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>\nThe user changed setting `Model Selection` from Gemini 3.1 Pro (High) to Gemini 3.1 Pro (Low). No need to comment on this change if the user doesn't ask about it. If reporting what model you are, please use a human readable name instead of the exact string.\n</USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>"}
